::PC_U01_L02_GP01::
Question ID: PC_U01_L02_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.1
Question:
What are the zeros of p(x) \= (x - 3)(x + 2)
{
~B. 3 and 2#The factor x + 2 gives zero -2, not 2.
~C. -3 and 2#This reverses the signs from both factors.
=A. 3 and -2#Correct. Setting each factor equal to zero gives x \= 3 and x \= -2.
~D. -3 and -2#The factor x - 3 gives zero 3, not -3.\}

::PC_U01_L02_GP02::
Question ID: PC_U01_L02_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.1
Question:
A polynomial has even degree and a positive leading coefficient. Which end behavior is expected
{
=A. Both ends up#Correct. Even degree and positive leading coefficient means the graph rises on both ends.
~B. Both ends down#Both ends down matches even degree with a negative leading coefficient.
~C. Left end up and right end down#That end behavior matches an odd degree polynomial with negative leading coefficient.
~D. Left end down and right end up#That end behavior matches an odd degree polynomial with positive leading coefficient.\}

::PC_U01_L02_GP03::
Question ID: PC_U01_L02_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.2
Question:
In p(x) = (x - 1)²(x + 4), what does the exponent 2 on (x - 1) indicate
{
~B. The degree is 2 only.#The whole polynomial has degree 3 because the exponents add to 3.
~C. The graph crosses the x-axis at x \= 1.#An even multiplicity usually means the graph touches and turns at the zero.
=A. The zero x \= 1 has even multiplicity.#Correct. The exponent 2 shows that x \= 1 has even multiplicity.
~D. The y-intercept is 2.#Multiplicity does not identify the y-intercept.\}

::PC_U01_L02_GP04::
Question ID: PC_U01_L02_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.3
Question:
A polynomial model for height has zeros at t = 0 and t = 6. What can the zero t = 6 represent in context
{
~A. The y-intercept is 6.#A y-intercept occurs at t \= 0, not necessarily at t \= 6.
~B. The maximum height is 6.#A zero identifies an input where the output is 0, not the maximum height.
~D. The model has degree 6.#A zero at t \= 6 does not determine the degree of the polynomial.
=C. The height is 0 at 6 seconds.#Correct. A zero of a height model means the height value is 0 at that input.\}

::PC_U01_L02_GP05::
Question ID: PC_U01_L02_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.1
Question:
Which polynomial has degree 5
{
~A. p(x) \= 4x² - 2x + 1#The highest exponent is 3, so the degree is 3.
=B. p(x) \= (x - 2)²(x + 1)³#Correct. The exponents add to 5.
~C. p(x) \= x²(x - 1)²#The total degree is 4, not 5.
~D. p(x) \= 5x - 7#The highest exponent is 1, so the degree is 1.\}
